WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.099
Hi, my name is Rob Phillips and I'm from Caltech. And I'm

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.966
here at the Marine Biological Laboratory in Woods Hole. And

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:25.866
I was here to record another iBio event in a certain sense, and

00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:28.432
we thought it would be a great opportunity to take advantage

00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:31.232
of the fact that I'm here with my mom on her 80th birthday.

00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:37.432
And so, what we're going to do is talk about our joint family

00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:41.099
adventure as I worked my way through science and I think

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.766
it will probably be interesting to people, since as you're going to find out,

00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:47.332
it was a bit of a wild adventure and it required a huge

00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:51.999
amount of parental support. So I thought I would just start

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.666
out with an easy question that I think all the listeners

00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:59.232
are really dying to know, which is what was your best

00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:00.699
birthday present ever?

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.399
You! Of course.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.466
How's that work?

00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:06.999
That works just fine. Perfect.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.432
So all kidding aside, so what we're going to do is maybe just take

00:01:11.433 --> 00:01:16.032
a little bit of a travel through the years, say between 9th grade,

00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:20.532
I guess, which is when I got a D in algebra, to maybe

00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:23.099
when I got my job at Brown University, which was the

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.866
end of our financial dependence on you guys, which is when I

00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:28.732
was 33 years old. So you know, that's hard.

00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:33.099
We grew up in a house full of books and I've always been a

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.666
great reader. I'm kind of curious what you think the role of books was

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:40.899
in our upbringing and also in all 3 of us, there's 3 kids,

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.066
and all of us are big readers.

00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:46.699
Well I think it started when you were all young, and I read you,

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.232
each of you, every single night. Even sometimes when we went out

00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:57.332
before sitters. So I was always a reader, my parents

00:01:57.333 --> 00:02:01.032
were always readers, and obviously books were hugely

00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:05.032
important to me personally. And I could tell from reading to

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:09.299
you, that you really enjoyed it and you took up books in a

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.199
big way. And it was something we shared, and I wanted to

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.032
just inject something, what a thrill it was for me to see when you

00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:21.199
had your two children, how you would read to them every

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.066
night. And sometimes when I would be visiting you, even when

00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:26.299
Casey was old enough certainly to read, when he was probably

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:31.632
13 or something. You two were reading the trilogy..

00:02:31.633 --> 00:02:32.799
Yeah, Tolkien.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:33.632
Tolkien trilogy.

00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:36.566
We also read Haroun and the Sea of Stories from uh..

00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:39.699
Right, so I thought, this is something that just went on.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.099
So obviously, we were big into books. I also remember going to

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.132
lots of used bookstores with you.

00:02:46.133 --> 00:02:50.299
Right, I was going to come to that. So by April 30th, 1997,

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.299
that's a Saturday, which is the day that I discovered that I was

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.766
going to do science. When I was given a lecture about how

00:02:57.767 --> 00:03:01.032
Eratosthenes figured out the radius of the Earth. And in a

00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:04.299
nutshell, somebody could tell me the radius of the Earth

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:08.199
but in a nutshell, what I learned was this clever guy measured

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.799
the radius of the Earth by looking at the shadow of the

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.366
noon day sun in two different places on the Earth and

00:03:13.367 --> 00:03:16.299
knowing the distance between the two places. And that

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.266
endows this question of realism, because it shows you how

00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:23.532
trigonometry, geometry, clever thinking, optics, you know all these

00:03:23.533 --> 00:03:26.866
things get merged. And above all, what it shows is that science

00:03:26.867 --> 00:03:29.632
is a relationship between one person and nature.

00:03:29.633 --> 00:03:32.632
There's no authorities. I don't like authorities. There's

00:03:32.633 --> 00:03:35.632
no books. There's no rules. There's no regulations.

00:03:35.633 --> 00:03:38.199
It's just you and your question. That's what Eratosthenes

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.566
was. That's what blew my mind. Before that, I thought

00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:42.699
science as kind of stupid, because I thought it was a bunch of

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.532
memorization of facts, like French kings, you know. I thought

00:03:46.533 --> 00:03:49.599
science was like that. The next morning, I told you and dad

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.566
that I was going to do science. And also told you I wasn't

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:55.499
gonna go to college, that I was going to leave high school

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.566
after 11th grade. On books, do you remember right after

00:03:59.567 --> 00:04:02.999
that, you and I went up to LA and we went used book shopping?

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.532
Yes we did -- And we came back with like, 200 books. I

00:04:05.533 --> 00:04:08.599
was just curious if you had anything to say, why did you do that?

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.032
And what was your take on that?  -- Because it was fun!

00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:13.032
Because we were together and we were doing our book thing.

00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:16.066
And I was always amazed because you were after books

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:24.032
I knew nothing about, all these theoretical physics titles00:04:19.07]
that were just totally out of my range. And I could go and look

00:04:24.033 --> 00:04:27.132
at the things I liked, which was fiction, nonfiction, whatever.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.932
But wander around with you, which is great fun

00:04:29.933 --> 00:04:31.932
to do that. What a kick!

00:04:31.933 --> 00:04:37.199
Yeah, so in that April I was 16 years old. And three months later

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.799
I basically left high school after 11th grade with my degree

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:45.799
and shortly thereafter, one of my good friends, which I'm sure you remember

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:50.699
very well, was killed in an accident. And that really established

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.632
that I was no -- no way that I was going to go to college.

00:04:52.633 --> 00:04:55.332
How does that relate? I mean, why does the death

00:04:55.333 --> 00:04:58.399
-- which I know is a horribly sorrowful time, but why that?

00:04:58.400 --> 00:04:59.532
Why not go to college?

00:04:59.533 --> 00:05:02.099
Yeah, I don't know if you remember. But I had a poster

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:06.432
in my room of Jack London, and it's a picture of him on

00:05:06.433 --> 00:05:09.266
his boat, and it said "I would rather be ashes than dust." And

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:12.299
it talks about being a fiery meteor, rather than a sleepy

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:18.532
planet or something like that. And I just read Irving Stone's book,

00:05:18.533 --> 00:05:21.399
Jack London: His Life, Sailor on Horseback. So it was the

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.799
juxtaposition of that book which showed me the life of Jack London,

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.432
which was really so amazing. And then seeing the death of Lance

00:05:29.433 --> 00:05:35.666
at age 17, you know, he was snuffed out. And it made me realize,

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:37.999
you know, today's kind of a good day to think about it, it's

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.299
a birthday. At this point, I've reached an age where birthdays

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.899
I find kind of sad, rather than happy days. Just in a certain

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.732
sense. And it's because you realize that life is short.

00:05:45.733 --> 00:05:50.866
And it just flies by. And then if you're unlucky, like Lance was,

00:05:50.867 --> 00:05:54.799
you can just have your life snubbed out. And what I felt

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.632
is that, you know, is going to college was just what you were supposed

00:05:58.633 --> 00:06:02.899
to do. And I guess I just felt that life is short, I better not do

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:06.299
what I'm supposed to do. And I should do what I should do.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:10.666
I just felt that I did not want to cave and just go off to UCSB

00:06:10.667 --> 00:06:13.232
for no reason. I would've just lived in Isla Vista

00:06:13.233 --> 00:06:15.266
and just gone to some stupid classes.

00:06:15.267 --> 00:06:18.166
Why did you even get to the point where you went up there,

00:06:18.167 --> 00:06:20.166
where you decided to go, where you got an apartment..

00:06:20.167 --> 00:06:26.032
Because you and dad insisted that you would let me do my deal

00:06:26.033 --> 00:06:31.232
if I got into a UC school. So I got into a UC school.

00:06:31.233 --> 00:06:35.199
Are you satisfied? So I got in and that was good enough.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.399
Then I had satisfied the parental requirement, but I had no

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:43.766
interest in going to UCSB or anywhere else. So I didn't.

00:06:43.767 --> 00:06:47.266
Well, I left home at 17. I moved up to LA shortly, but

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:49.566
the real thing that happened was I went off on a boat.

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:53.399
I sailed 3000 miles. And what I wanted to ask you about,

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.332
has in a certain sense to do with how I feel about my kids

00:06:56.333 --> 00:07:00.032
and the adventures they have. I was 17 when I left the port

00:07:00.033 --> 00:07:04.199
at Harbor Island on that boat. And I'm kind of curious, you know,

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.666
we set off. There was no cell phones, I didn't talk to you for three months.

00:07:07.667 --> 00:07:12.166
I sent home stupid letters that came by snail mail. I'm curious about

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:16.032
parental fear in the face of adventure. Because there was lots of it

00:07:16.033 --> 00:07:18.966
that we might touch on, but what did you and dad have to say

00:07:18.967 --> 00:07:22.032
about, you know, these things that were ostensibly scary?

00:07:22.033 --> 00:07:25.299
And I remember when you -- standing there watching you,

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:29.099
literally sail away. And of course, I was all teary and I thought

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:32.366
-- I think maybe I was just stupid and I didn't realize how

00:07:32.367 --> 00:07:36.799
much could happen. I was also amazed at the letters that you

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:40.666
wrote home. Because you were reading all of Shakespeare,

00:07:40.667 --> 00:07:44.699
as I recall. Trying to go through it one at a time. And commenting

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:47.266
on that. Commenting on what you were seeing and feeling.

00:07:47.267 --> 00:07:50.466
Although, we have to be honest, they were incredibly

00:07:50.467 --> 00:07:53.232
embarrassingly pretentious. I mean, I was trying out

00:07:53.233 --> 00:07:55.999
my Shakespearean vocabulary and looked like a moron.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.532
I know, but as a mom, it's okay.

00:07:59.533 --> 00:08:02.799
Because I thought, here's a kid who's really going out there,

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.332
because once you told us, and you told us the day that you

00:08:06.333 --> 00:08:08.399
were supposed to -- and we were going to get into the car and drive you

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.132
to Santa Barbara that you were not going to college,

00:08:10.133 --> 00:08:14.132
right then is when you think, is this really going to work or not?

00:08:14.133 --> 00:08:17.999
Are you really going to make a life for yourself? When did you

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.999
-- when you finally said, okay, I'm going to go. I'm going to take courses

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:25.032
now through the University of Minnesota. What brought that on you?

00:08:25.033 --> 00:08:27.366
So now I'm going to go back, I'm going to have to go back

00:08:27.367 --> 00:08:29.766
and do something rather more traditional, in order to do

00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:30.966
what I think I want to do.

00:08:30.967 --> 00:08:34.799
So first of all, let's remind you and me, that was 6 years

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.132
after high school. In that intervening 6 years, you know, I worked

00:08:39.133 --> 00:08:42.966
at a computer store, I worked at Alpha Microsystems and repaired

00:08:42.967 --> 00:08:46.866
hard disk drives, went on an 18,000 mile trip, I took 6 months,

00:08:46.867 --> 00:08:49.866
I was an electrician for 4 years, I crawled around in attics

00:08:49.867 --> 00:08:53.266
in Santa Barbara and La Jolla. And I was studying all

00:08:53.267 --> 00:08:56.132
the time. And what happened was, I was on a 3 month trip to Europe,

00:08:56.133 --> 00:09:00.632
and I was sitting on a train. And a guy from Westinghouse,

00:09:00.633 --> 00:09:03.799
an executive was sitting next to me, his name was Donald

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:07.066
Trexler. I met him once in my life, and he told me I was an idiot.

00:09:07.067 --> 00:09:10.266
I basically told him that I had a resume that said I had read

00:09:10.267 --> 00:09:12.732
this and that book, and I had learned this and that physics,

00:09:12.733 --> 00:09:15.532
and so on. And I said I sent it to Beckman Instruments and

00:09:15.533 --> 00:09:17.899
all these places, and they basically didn't even answer me.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.932
And he said, well, you're an idiot. Go get your union card.

00:09:20.933 --> 00:09:22.866
So what happened was, I came back to San Diego and I

00:09:22.867 --> 00:09:25.966
went to the UCSD library and I found this book called, How to

00:09:25.967 --> 00:09:27.432
Get a College Degree Without Going to College.

00:09:27.433 --> 00:09:30.466
And there were four universities I found that had these

00:09:30.467 --> 00:09:33.832
sort of university without walls programs, where you could earn

00:09:33.833 --> 00:09:37.566
a degree without going to college. Because I just think that

00:09:37.567 --> 00:09:41.232
I'm not suited -- I mean, I'm a failure as a classroom student.

00:09:41.233 --> 00:09:45.766
You know, I don't like tests, I don't like the notion of one person

00:09:45.767 --> 00:09:48.766
telling other people what they're supposed to know.

00:09:48.767 --> 00:09:50.732
That's interesting. I don't remember the gentleman's story

00:09:50.733 --> 00:09:53.032
on the train. That's really interesting. So that's really what

00:09:53.033 --> 00:09:56.199
-- That's really what happened. And then, the connection to

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.566
grad school is because Chuck Campbell, who was at

00:09:59.567 --> 00:10:02.799
Minnesota. You know, everyone was kind of against him

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.066
admitting a physics student. But he said, let the guy fail.

00:10:05.067 --> 00:10:07.532
And you know, to me, that's one of the main philosophies of life,

00:10:07.533 --> 00:10:11.066
let people fail. Give them a chance to fail, because we're very

00:10:11.067 --> 00:10:14.632
conservative. And I like to use sports analogies, you know all my

00:10:14.633 --> 00:10:18.466
heroes in surfing and snowboarding, they pay. They pay with getting

00:10:18.467 --> 00:10:21.332
hurt. And the reason is because they're right on the edge

00:10:21.333 --> 00:10:23.632
And that's the kind of science that I like. So anyway, he said

00:10:23.633 --> 00:10:26.799
let the guy fail. And after I talked to him, he said, you know

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.499
you should go to grad school. And I had no clue what grad school

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:35.432
was. None. And I said, okay, I'll try it out. And so I applied

00:10:35.433 --> 00:10:39.566
and I got into some of the places, such as Wash U and UCSD. Yeah.

00:10:39.567 --> 00:10:40.432
So you did.

00:10:40.433 --> 00:10:43.532
Yeah. Plus, I mentioned at the beginning, until the age of 33,

00:10:43.533 --> 00:10:46.299
we were still getting financial help from you and dad. So

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:49.232
I'm curious to hear your thoughts about the grad student

00:10:49.233 --> 00:10:52.566
trajectory. You know, the hardship financially. And the

00:10:52.567 --> 00:10:55.899
not knowing where you're going to get a job. You know,

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:58.899
lot of graduate students and postdocs, they go exactly through

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:01.032
what we went through. Where you wonder, am I going to get

00:11:01.033 --> 00:11:04.032
a professorship? You remember, I interviewed on Wall Street.

00:11:04.033 --> 00:11:05.566
So what are your thoughts about that?

00:11:05.567 --> 00:11:09.799
Well, I think if you can manage it, obviously. You try to manage

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:12.399
it. It's your children, you want them to get the best you can

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:16.432
for them. So sure, it's not easy financially. I think even

00:11:16.433 --> 00:11:20.266
more important is the wandering about in your case, where you

00:11:20.267 --> 00:11:23.532
don't go in the traditional way. And I remember when you were trying

00:11:23.533 --> 00:11:27.132
-- you were even getting your first postdoc, you saying something

00:11:27.133 --> 00:11:29.166
like, oh they're going to want someone who went to Harvard,

00:11:29.167 --> 00:11:33.432
who came up the traditional way. And I'd say, oh no, I'm sure things

00:11:33.433 --> 00:11:36.432
will be alright. Not knowing, of course. But I had a great

00:11:36.433 --> 00:11:40.366
-- I just thought you did a great job. I wasn't sure it was going to come, but

00:11:40.367 --> 00:11:41.766
it's hard!

00:11:41.767 --> 00:11:45.032
I would just say that there's a lot of emphasis on that.

00:11:45.033 --> 00:11:48.866
The blue blood aspects, it does have a big effect on

00:11:48.867 --> 00:11:54.166
things. What about parenting? I just like to a little bit come back to

00:11:54.167 --> 00:11:57.132
these things of, you know, I'm thinking about the many

00:11:57.133 --> 00:12:01.132
rough steps along the way. So you know, the -- I mentioned earlier

00:12:01.133 --> 00:12:04.499
that Lance got killed, and you remember that his brother, Joe,

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:06.699
who was also my dear friend, drowned in the ocean

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:12.932
not too long after that. And again, some of these adventures,

00:12:12.933 --> 00:12:17.066
the dangers, I guess I'm just -- I'm very curious to hear how

00:12:17.067 --> 00:12:19.599
it played out. And I think you may remember a story from

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:21.866
Caltech. I'm not going to mention who the grad student was,

00:12:21.867 --> 00:12:24.999
but you were visiting and one of my grad students made a remark

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:27.632
about how all this stuff that we're talking about now is actually me telling

00:12:27.633 --> 00:12:31.132
stories about myself. And you bit his head off, because you

00:12:31.133 --> 00:12:35.032
lived it. And again, this isn't about glorifying anything that happened,

00:12:35.033 --> 00:12:38.166
it's actually quite the opposite. I'm still just very curious about

00:12:38.167 --> 00:12:41.832
how I would've been quite fearful if I had been in your

00:12:41.833 --> 00:12:43.966
boat. I'm not sure if I would've been able to handle it.

00:12:43.967 --> 00:12:46.466
Having my kids do that.

00:12:46.467 --> 00:12:50.732
I don't know how I handled it. I wasn't as fearful as I was

00:12:50.733 --> 00:12:53.332
when I look back on it, I think, you know, I probably should've been

00:12:53.333 --> 00:12:56.232
scared out of my mind. I think I took it rather well.

00:12:56.233 --> 00:12:58.299
Yeah, there's no doubt about it.

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:01.699
About parenting, I think it's the one thing in life that nobody ever teaches

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:06.499
anyone. And it's something you -- it's just like trial and error.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:09.432
You muddle through, you would know that, you muddle through.

00:13:09.433 --> 00:13:14.666
But certainly, the way you went about living your life and starting

00:13:14.667 --> 00:13:18.266
out on your trajectory was scary. And a lot of times I

00:13:18.267 --> 00:13:21.499
was afraid and obviously when you were gone, I missed you

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:25.799
terribly. But as I say, I think you really showed rather quickly

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:27.566
that you were gonna make it.

00:13:27.567 --> 00:13:31.499
The thing that's easy to take for granted and that's not obvious

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:35.599
I think to people is that there's a bit of nuance about how

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:38.199
these stories play out that have to do with the family

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:43.666
that surrounds you. And so, here at the MBL, I see people with

00:13:43.667 --> 00:13:47.366
families and you can kind of  get a sense that, okay, that's somebody

00:13:47.367 --> 00:13:50.666
where there's some magic going on in that household. And

00:13:50.667 --> 00:13:55.166
you know, I've said it before, but this is a pretty great day.

00:13:55.167 --> 00:13:59.432
Because it's your 80th birthday and it's my 54th birthday.

00:13:59.433 --> 00:14:01.866
And yesterday was my daughter and your granddaughter's

00:14:01.867 --> 00:14:05.766
birthday. It's a time for reflection, since we're all together.

00:14:05.767 --> 00:14:09.732
And also, you know, I'm here as the director of the Physiology

00:14:09.733 --> 00:14:12.366
course, which is a dream come true and an impossibility

00:14:12.367 --> 00:14:15.432
to even 10 years ago even imagine that I would have that

00:14:15.433 --> 00:14:19.566
privilege. And so, I guess what I would say about it is

00:14:19.567 --> 00:14:23.332
that, you know, I would like the viewers to be aware

00:14:23.333 --> 00:14:27.999
of it is that it took a lot of support. Financial support,

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:30.199
of course, but I'm not really talking about that. I'm talking about the

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.999
emotional, the psychological, the intellectual support

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:37.399
that you and dad gave all of us, but me especially

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:43.332
along this crazy adventure. And the adventure continues.

00:14:43.333 --> 00:14:45.732
And our adventures continue together.

00:14:45.733 --> 00:14:46.399
Let's hope.

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:50.999
Yeah, and I guess I'm looking forward having a nice rest of

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:53.699
today with you. And I love you very much.

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:57.100
I love you very much. I'm very proud of you. Very grateful.

